A||||5||DreXteR||Where 2 Buy?   Advise||||5||
Z||000000||DreXteR||01-12-2001||04:32 PM||||I've almost decide what to do with my engine rebuild.  Now I looking for parts price to see if woth it.<P>Well here goes:<BR>GM Performance camshaft 12363220  $90.34<BR>GM Performance 3.1 cranck        $339.90 (is this the same cranck that have any 3.1 stock engine?) What is the diff of this crank vs the regular one?<P>GM Performance valve springs(recomended by GM)    $49.20<BR>GM Performance Valve rtainers (recomended by GM)    $27.24<P>I went to wiseco web and didnt find any 3.1 or 3.2 v6 60* pistons there. I have to call for the info?  If someboy have it, please post it. Or if know how much it will cost, can be appreciated.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  3.1 or 3.2 flat forged pistons.<P>If there something that dont worth it or be changed, plaese advise me.<P>Thanx.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||01-12-2001||08:26 PM||||I'm surprised this post has gone unanswered.<P>The GM performance 12363220 is an awesome camshaft for your engine. I have that camshaft in my 3.4-liter engine (not running yet). I'm glad to see you got the matching springs and retainers and you should have new lifters also. The prices you got are very good. I paid more. You cannot reuse your old lifters on a new camshaft or you will ruin it very quickly. <P>There is no difference between that crankshaft you have and the ones in the production 1989-up 3.1-liter engines. They have the same cast nodular construction. The nice thing about that crankshaft in the GM performance catalog is that it is a new crankshaft not a refurbished crankshaft. Refurbished crankshafts have mains and journals that are ground down, sometimes too much for a performance motor. <P>The only pistons that I know you can get for this engine are the Silvolite 1486 dished pistons and most guys here on the forum claim that gives you an 8.8-1 compression ratio although that has not been proven yet. The only way to prove this is to have the combustion chambers cc'ed. The Silvolite 1486 pistons are hypereutectic pistons. <P>I may be wrong but when I looked into getting high compression forged pistons for a 3.1-liter motor (that I almost did) the only way I could get those was to get custom made pistons. Custom forged pistons will probably cost over $500 too.<P>With an 8.8-1 compression ratio, ported manifolds, GM 12363220 camshaft, and the 3.1 crankshaft your fiero will approximately get to 60 mph in 6.64 seconds (7.54 automatic) and run the quarter mile in 14.71 @ 93.7 mph (automatic15.64 @ 91.3 mph) seconds with the 5-speed transmission. That motor will also make about 163 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 198-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. <P>Spending all that money you may want to look into getting a 3.4-liter Camaro motor. This is what I did. I used the pistons for the 3.4-liter double overhead camshaft motor in my push rod motor and got a 9.7-1 compression ratio. I bought a core engine and had it bored .020” and I had it zero-deck and squared. You have to relocate the starter to the other side of the block though. I bought a drilling jig from George Miller <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A>  and drilled them myself. It was very easy with that jig. If you have an 85-87 v6 than you have to get a neutrally balanced flexplate or flywheel depending on what transmission you have. You also have to do some other things to the motor to make it work and you can find out about it here. <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html</A>   You can bolt up everything that is on your 2.8-liter engine to the 3.4-liter engine and anyone looking at it will never be able to tell the difference from the 2.8-liter engine. The 3.4-liter Camaro engine has a better oiling system than the 2.8-liter engines have. <P>You can buy a used 3.4-liter engine from a 93-95 Camaro that is in good running condition and install it into your fiero stock like many other members have done here. The stock Camaro 3.4-liter engine has a 9.0-1 compression ratio and you can expect about 166 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 208 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000-3500 rpm in stock form with ported exhaust manifolds. That is good enough to get you to 60 mph in 6.5 seconds with the 5-speed manual transmission and through the quarter mile in 14.57 seconds @ 94.5 mph. With the automatic transmission you will get to 60 mph in 7.2 seconds and the quarter mile in 15.37 seconds @ 92.2 mph. <P>If you get the 3.1-liter crankshaft for your 2.8-liter motor than you definitely should have the bottom end balanced because it’s likely to be off. You could get away with not balancing the bottom end with a stock rebuild but because you are putting a different crankshaft in your motor than what came with your motor you should have it balanced. It will cost you around $175-$250 to have that motor balanced. I also recommend before you do anymore work on that motor that you get the “how to rebuild your GM v-6 60 engine” by Motorbooks International and written by Tom Currao. You can get it from Barns and Nobles book stores for about $15-$20.<P>I got those figures from Dyno2000 and Dragstrip2000. If you would like me to make up some more for you than contact me. <P>Here are some web-sites for you to check out. <BR> <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/gpxss/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/gpxss/index.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.leaderind.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.leaderind.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.users.uswest.net/~racing2win/mild2wld.htm#Conversions" TARGET=_blank>http://www.users.uswest.net/~racing2win/mild2wld.htm#Conversions</A>  <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/Page1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/Page1.html</A>  <A HREF="http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/engine/engine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/engine/engine.html</A>  <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/e-conv.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/e-conv.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html</A>  A good one for hard to find high performance 3.4 parts. <A HREF="http://www.s-series.org/htm/windstorm/project-windstorm-stage3.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.s-series.org/htm/windstorm/project-windstorm-stage3.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://www.s-series.org/htm/tech/60DegreeCams/60DegreeCams.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.s-series.org/htm/tech/60DegreeCams/60DegreeCams.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt</A>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html#Turbochargers" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html#Turbochargers</A>  This one is the mother of fiero sites to see, let it load first. <P>Man that was allot of work!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DreXteR||01-14-2001||01:13 PM||||88formula, thanks for the advise. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>In the 3.4 swap the hp goal is the same with 3.1 that I will make. And 9.0 it's too high, In the future I want to make it turbo.  I need a low compression engine. Thats why the 3.2 pistons, they have 8.3:1 !! <A HREF="http://www.kb-silvolite.com/slvpg23.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kb-silvolite.com/slvpg23.htm</A>  ,<BR>The 1424 is a 3.2 flat pistion with the low compression I need for the turbo. But in order to use it the block had to bore to .060, and I don't know if the block will handle 15psi of pressure and 300+ HP.<P>Btw, a Z34 engine will fit in the fiero?  Or the parts can be used in the 3.4 camaro engine?  The HP of the Z34 is 210 vs 160 of the Camaro!  Thats funny!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Same category but dif power.  hehe  Strange<P><BR>Well, in order to me make the 3.4 swap 160 hp isnt deal, the power of the Z34 is more attractive.  Besides, the Z34 engine have 9.2:1 comp. =(  I will need to take it down to @least 8.6:1, can this be done?<P>Help!, I need a F*** direction to go in the rebuild!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Thaks<P>DreXteR||||reg||3
Z||000003||DreXteR||01-14-2001||01:16 PM||||OH man!  I cant see my reply!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||01-14-2001||02:09 PM||||I would have to agree here, in that you should be looking into a 3.4 swap instead of a 3.1 rebuild. I am currently (well, just started) completely rebuilding my 86 GT, include swapping out to a 3.4L Camaro engine. Only things I am doing is custom headers, ported heads, more aggressive cam. You will, not now but in the future, have less problems if you use an engine like a 3.4 and as opposed to trying to get alot of HP out of a small 2.8 block with 3.1 capacity. The 3.4 swap uses only the 3.4 block, heads and intake from the old 2.8 are bolted up to the new engine. I have a complete list of things I am doing to the engine on my website: <A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>www.kdsi.net/~sean</A> <P>P.S. Those are pretty good prices on the cam and crank your looking at. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto (3.4 Conversion in Progress)<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||01-14-2001||06:06 PM||||The Z34 engine used in the Lumina has 210 horsepower because that motor has four valves per a cylinder and double over head camshafts.<P>I did the math on those 1486 pistons with the 12-cc head volume and I got 8.39-1 compression. That is if the fiero heads are 57 cc.<BR>||||reg||1
